WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.377
Here's a question that sounds simple.

2
00:00:05.377 --> 00:00:12.667
A pipeline, the robot on GitHub that runs plan and apply for you, has to get into AWS, Amazon's cloud.

3
00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:14.113
It's not a person.

4
00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:17.920
It has no phone for a code and no password in its head.

5
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.481
So how does AWS know it's allowed in?

6
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:23.830
Let's leave computers for a minute.

7
00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:27.309
You're away for two weeks while a builder works on your house.

8
00:00:27.309 --> 00:00:28.936
The quick way to let them in?

9
00:00:28.936 --> 00:00:30.619
A spare key under the doormat.

10
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.205
On day one, it works.

11
00:00:32.205 --> 00:00:35.016
The builder lifts the mat, and in they go.

12
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.385
It still works in a year.

13
00:00:37.385 --> 00:00:41.061
And it works for anyone who lifts the mat, builder or not.

14
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.012
Under the doormat isn't really a hiding place.

15
00:00:44.012 --> 00:00:47.741
It's the first place anyone looks, right after the flowerpot.

16
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:50.864
The pipeline has its own doormat.

17
00:00:50.864 --> 00:00:57.997
The quick way is an AWS access key, saved as a GitHub secret, a hidden setting the pipeline can read.

18
00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:00.767
That key works until someone deletes it.

19
00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:02.413
Nothing about it expires.

20
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:08.303
So if it ever leaks, the stolen copy stays useful for months, quietly, for whoever has it.

21
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:14.404
Wouldn't it be nice if the pipeline got a key that opened only one room, and stopped working on its own?

22
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.388
A hotel does it differently.

23
00:01:17.388 --> 00:01:19.304
You walk up to the front desk.

24
00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:20.534
You show your ID.

25
00:01:20.534 --> 00:01:27.693
The desk doesn't know you, but it knows the office that stamped that ID, and it can tell a real stamp from a fake.

26
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:32.673
Then it checks a second thing: is the name on that ID on tonight's booking list?

27
00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:33.505
Both yes?

28
00:01:33.505 --> 00:01:35.207
It hands you a key card.

29
00:01:35.207 --> 00:01:38.114
The card opens room twelve, nothing else.

30
00:01:38.114 --> 00:01:41.021
And at checkout, it simply stops working.

31
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.717
Nobody had to remember to cancel it.

32
00:01:43.717 --> 00:01:45.395
The card cancels itself.

33
00:01:45.533 --> 00:01:46.438
Let's map it.

34
00:01:46.438 --> 00:01:49.152
You, the guest, are the pipeline's job.

35
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:50.970
The ID office is GitHub.

36
00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.972
It signs a pass for the job, called a token.

37
00:01:53.972 --> 00:01:56.564
It's short lived, and it soon expires.

38
00:01:56.733 --> 00:01:58.279
The front desk is AWS.

39
00:01:58.279 --> 00:02:03.829
The booking list is the role's trust policy, the part that says who may use it.

40
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.638
The key card is a set of short-lived AWS credentials.

41
00:02:07.638 --> 00:02:11.757
And room twelve is what the role may touch, its permissions.

42
00:02:12.333 --> 00:02:16.258
That whole handshake has a name: OIDC, OpenID Connect.

43
00:02:16.258 --> 00:02:22.800
It's a standard that lets GitHub prove who a job is, with a signed pass that soon expires.

44
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.907
Let's look at one real pass.

45
00:02:24.907 --> 00:02:28.586
It holds claims, named fields that GitHub vouches for.

46
00:02:28.586 --> 00:02:29.676
Two matter here.

47
00:02:29.867 --> 00:02:35.020
aud, the audience, says who the pass is meant for: AWS's sign-in service.

48
00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.608
sub, the subject, names the repo and the context the job runs in.

49
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.175
So the job asks GitHub for a pass.

50
00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:44.580
It carries the pass to AWS.

51
00:02:44.733 --> 00:02:49.670
Check one: is GitHub's signature real, and is the audience right?

52
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:49.973
Yes.

53
00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:53.491
Check two: does the sub match the trust policy?

54
00:02:53.491 --> 00:02:54.563
Your repo, yes.

55
00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:58.928
Then AWS hands back short-lived credentials, and plan runs.

56
00:02:58.928 --> 00:03:00.848
No key was stored anywhere.

57
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.064
Now let's be skeptical.

58
00:03:03.064 --> 00:03:05.235
AWS trusts GitHub's signature.

59
00:03:05.235 --> 00:03:10.444
But GitHub signs passes for every repo in the world, strangers included.

60
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:15.765
So a stranger's workflow asks GitHub for a pass, and gets a real one.

61
00:03:15.765 --> 00:03:17.188
Does AWS let it in?

62
00:03:17.188 --> 00:03:18.385
Pause and guess.

63
00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.361
The signature checks out.

64
00:03:22.361 --> 00:03:23.896
That pass is genuine.

65
00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:29.979
But the sub names the stranger's repo, and the trust policy names only yours.

66
00:03:29.979 --> 00:03:36.581
AWS refuses, with this error: Not authorized to perform sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity.

67
00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:39.892
A perfectly real ID, with the wrong name on it.

68
00:03:39.892 --> 00:03:43.588
So the point is: the signature says who wrote the pass.

69
00:03:43.588 --> 00:03:45.336
The sub says who it's for.

70
00:03:45.336 --> 00:03:46.680
The desk needs both.

71
00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:50.487
And the desk reads names character for character.

72
00:03:50.487 --> 00:03:52.707
Older repos write the sub like this.

73
00:03:52.867 --> 00:03:58.643
Repos created after July 15, 2026 put numeric IDs in it, like this.

74
00:03:58.643 --> 00:04:02.608
The lab trusts both forms, for your repo only.

75
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.016
So when you see that error, don't blame the pipeline.

76
00:04:06.016 --> 00:04:07.229
Check the sub first.

77
00:04:08.533 --> 00:04:09.620
One more thing.

78
00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.096
A key card is only as safe as the room it opens.

79
00:04:13.096 --> 00:04:14.761
So keep the role small.

80
00:04:14.933 --> 00:04:19.548
The lab's role may manage only buckets whose names start with hello-devops.

81
00:04:19.733 --> 00:04:23.964
It may also read its own IAM setup, so that plan works.

82
00:04:23.964 --> 00:04:27.811
IAM is AWS's service that decides who may do what.

83
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.607
And it cannot change IAM.

84
00:04:29.607 --> 00:04:30.956
Why does that matter?

85
00:04:30.956 --> 00:04:38.089
If it could, a pull request could edit the pipeline's own permissions, and turn its key card into a master key.

86
00:04:38.267 --> 00:04:41.231
A card that can upgrade itself isn't a card.

87
00:04:41.231 --> 00:04:44.061
So you apply IAM changes from your laptop.

88
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.685
Even asking for a pass is limited.

89
00:04:46.685 --> 00:04:50.851
Only the two jobs that log in to AWS get id-token: write.

90
00:04:50.851 --> 00:04:53.628
The checks job can only read the repo.

91
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.832
So here's the moral of the story.

92
00:04:56.832 --> 00:05:00.033
A key that never expires is a key under the doormat.

93
00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:02.372
Let the pipeline borrow one that does.

94
00:05:02.533 --> 00:05:03.940
GitHub signs a pass.

95
00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:08.443
AWS checks the signature, then the sub, character for character.

96
00:05:08.443 --> 00:05:10.835
It hands back a card that expires.

97
00:05:10.835 --> 00:05:13.227
And the card opens one small room.

98
00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.230
A quick check before you go.

99
00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:20.153
Your plan job passes its checks, then fails with this error.

100
00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:21.722
What do you check first?

101
00:05:21.722 --> 00:05:22.768
Pause and think.

102
00:05:24.933 --> 00:05:25.438
The sub.

103
00:05:25.438 --> 00:05:30.237
The name on the pass has to match the booking list, character for character.
